       ##iinncclluuddee <<ssttddlliibb..hh>>

       [[[[nnoorreettuurrnn]]]] vvooiidd aabboorrtt((vvooiidd));;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The aabboorrtt() function first unblocks the SSIIGGAABBRRTT signal, and then raises
       that  signal  for  the calling process (as though rraaiissee(3) was called).
       This results in the abnormal termination  of  the  process  unless  the
       SSIIGGAABBRRTT  signal  is  caught and the signal handler does not return (see
       lloonnggjjmmpp(3)).

       If the SSIIGGAABBRRTT signal is ignored, or caught by a handler that  returns,
       the aabboorrtt() function will still terminate the process.  It does this by
       restoring the default disposition for SSIIGGAABBRRTT and then raising the sig‐
       nal for a second time.

       As  with  other  cases of abnormal termination the functions registered
       with aatteexxiitt(3) and oonn__eexxiitt(3) are not called.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The aabboorrtt() function never returns.

