SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       C11, POSIX.1‐2008.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       SVr4, POSIX.1‐2001, 4.3BSD, C89.

       Up until glibc 2.26, if the aabboorrtt() function caused process
       termination, all open streams were closed and flushed (as with
       ffcclloossee(3)).  However, in some cases this could result in deadlocks and
       data corruption.  Therefore, starting with glibc 2.27, aabboorrtt()
       terminates the process without flushing streams.  POSIX.1 permits
       either possible behavior, saying that aabboorrtt() "may include an attempt
       to effect fclose() on all open streams".

